export const KNOWLEDGE = "# Company (confirmed)\nEZTek Solutions, in business since 2000. Office: 1707 70th St, Brooklyn, NY 11204 (Bensonhurst). Phone: (888) 693-9835.\nLow-voltage systems integrator for residential, commercial, multifamily, educational and institutional properties in New York City.\n\n# Website pages\n/quote/ Get a quote: four short steps, shows a typical installed price range, books a free site survey in NYC, accepts photos and plans.\n/service/ Request service for an existing system. /care-plans/ Maintenance care plans. /portal/ Client login. /contact/ Contact. /partners/ For contractors and architects.\n\n# Services\n## Video surveillance (/services/video-surveillance/)\nIP cameras, recorders and cloud video for entrances, lobbies, garages and stores.\nWe plan camera positions from the incidents you want to resolve, not from a camera count. Every lens is aimed and tested from the recorder, and you can pull footage from your phone the same day.\nIncludes: Site survey with a camera coverage plan; IP cameras, NVRs and cloud video; License plate and people-counting cameras; Remote viewing on phone and desktop; Upgrades from analog and DVR systems; Footage export for police and insurance.\nSigns it is time: Footage is too blurry to identify a face; The recorder is full or nobody knows the password; Cameras go offline and nobody notices for weeks; You still use analog cameras and a DVR.\nQ: Can you reuse our existing cameras or cabling? A: Often, yes. Coax can carry IP cameras with adapters, and many recorders accept newer cameras. We test what is there during the survey and tell you what to keep.\nQ: How long is footage kept? A: Usually 30 days on site, or longer with cloud retention. We size the storage to the period you need.\nQ: Do you install cameras in apartment buildings with tenants in place? A: Yes. Most work is in common areas and we schedule noisy work during the day with notice to residents.\n## Access control (/services/access-control/)\nCard, fob, PIN and mobile credentials for doors, gates, elevators and garages.\nStop re-keying locks. We install readers, locks and controllers so you can add or remove a person in seconds and see who opened which door.\nIncludes: Fob, card, PIN and phone credentials; Cloud-managed and on-site systems; Electric strikes, maglocks and exit devices; Elevator and garage access; Tenant move-in and move-out programming; Door audit reports.\nSigns it is time: Keys and fobs of former tenants still work; Changing a code means a site visit; You cannot tell who entered after hours; Doors are propped open and nobody knows.\nQ: Can residents open the door with their phone? A: Yes. Most current systems support mobile credentials alongside fobs, so residents can use either.\nQ: What happens in a power outage? A: Controllers and locks run on backup batteries, and every door has a code-compliant way out. We design fail-safe or fail-secure by door.\n## Alarm & intrusion (/services/alarm-systems/)\nIntrusion detection, panic buttons and alarm monitoring for homes and businesses.\nDoor contacts, motion and glass-break sensors connected to a panel you can arm from your phone, with central-station monitoring when you want it.\nIncludes: Wired and wireless intrusion sensors; Panic and hold-up buttons; Phone arming and alerts; Central-station monitoring setup; Integration with cameras for verified alarms; Takeover of existing panels.\nSigns it is time: False alarms you have learned to ignore; An old keypad nobody knows how to use; No alerts when the store opens late; A monitoring contract you pay for and do not use.\nQ: Do I have to sign a monitoring contract? A: No. You can self-monitor through the app or add professional monitoring. We will explain both.\nQ: Can you take over the system another company installed? A: Usually, yes, if the panel is not locked by the previous installer. We check that during the survey.\n## Intercom & entry (/services/intercom-entry/)\nVideo intercoms, telephone entry and smartphone entry for lobbies and gates.\nReplace the buzzer that half the building ignores. Residents see the visitor on their phone and open the door from anywhere, and couriers get a code that works only for them.\nIncludes: Video intercom panels for lobbies and gates; Smartphone entry with no apartment wiring; Apartment stations and handsets; Delivery and courier codes; Directory management online; Upgrades of old audio intercoms.\nSigns it is time: Visitors cannot reach the tenant they want; Packages pile up because couriers cannot get in; Handsets in apartments are broken or missing; The panel shows names of people who left years ago.\nQ: Do residents need new wiring in their apartments? A: Not with smartphone-based entry. If residents want an in-unit station, we can reuse existing intercom wiring in most buildings.\nQ: Is there a monthly fee? A: Cloud intercoms usually have a per-building or per-unit service fee from the manufacturer. We show it in the proposal next to the install price.\n## Audio & video (/services/audio-video/)\nHome theaters, conference rooms, distributed audio and video walls.\nRooms that work on the first try. We design, install and program theaters, conference rooms and multi-room audio so one button does what people expect.\nIncludes: Home theaters and media rooms; Conference rooms and video conferencing; Distributed audio by zone; Video walls and digital signage; TV mounting with hidden cables; Calibration of picture and sound.\nSigns it is time: Every meeting starts with ten minutes of cables; Five remotes for one TV; Music plays in one room only; Screens in the lobby still show last year.\nQ: Can you work with our architect or designer? A: Yes. We provide speaker and screen layouts, backing and conduit requirements early so walls are built once.\nQ: Do you support Zoom Rooms and Microsoft Teams Rooms? A: Yes. We install certified room kits and test calls before handover.\n## Networks & cabling (/services/networking-cabling/)\nStructured cabling, fiber optics, Wi-Fi, racks and phone systems.\nEvery other system runs on the network. We pull, label and certify cabling, build the rack, and design Wi-Fi from a site survey instead of guesswork.\nIncludes: Cat6/Cat6A cabling, tested and certified; Fiber optic backbones and risers; Wi-Fi surveys and installation; Racks, patch panels and cable management; Switches, firewalls and VLANs; VoIP phone systems.\nSigns it is time: Wi-Fi drops in the same rooms every day; Nobody knows which cable goes where; The rack is a pile of patch cords; Moving an office desk takes a contractor visit.\nQ: Do you work during off-hours? A: Yes. Cutovers in offices and stores are usually scheduled at night or on weekends to avoid downtime.\nQ: Can you wire a new office before move-in? A: Yes. We coordinate with the general contractor and finish cabling, Wi-Fi and phones before the first day.\n## Building automation (/services/building-automation/)\nLighting control, motorized shades, HVAC integration and schedules.\nLights, shades and climate that follow the time of day and the people in the room. We keep it simple to use and easy to change later.\nIncludes: Lighting control and scenes; Motorized shades and drapery; Thermostat and HVAC integration; Occupancy and daylight sensors; Energy schedules for common areas; Keypads that make sense.\nSigns it is time: Hallway lights on all night; Shades nobody can reach; Every thermostat set differently; A smart home that only one person can use.\nQ: Can automation be added to an existing home? A: Yes. Wireless lighting and shade systems need little or no new wiring and can be installed room by room.\nQ: Will it save energy in common areas? A: Schedules and occupancy sensors usually cut runtime of lights and HVAC in hallways and amenity rooms. We show the settings so you can adjust them.\n## System integration (/services/system-integration/)\nOne control platform for lights, shades, AV, cameras, access and climate.\nSeparate systems become one. We program control processors and touch panels so the door, cameras, music, lights and climate answer to one app.\nIncludes: Control processors and touch panels; One app for every system; Custom scenes and automations; Integration of existing equipment; Remote access and updates; Documentation of every rule.\nSigns it is time: Six apps to run one home; Systems from different installers that ignore each other; A programmer who no longer answers; Scenes that broke after an update.\nQ: Can you take over programming from another integrator? A: If the project files are available or the system is not dealer-locked, yes. If not, we rebuild the program and document it for you.\nQ: Who owns the program? A: You do. We keep a backup in your portal so you are never locked to one dealer.\n## Maintenance & support (/services/maintenance-support/)\nRepairs, preventive maintenance, upgrades and remote support on care plans.\nMost problems are cheaper to prevent than to fix. Care plans give you scheduled inspections, monitoring and faster dispatch, and every request is tracked in the portal.\nIncludes: Troubleshooting and repairs; Preventive maintenance visits with reports; Firmware and security updates; Device health monitoring; Remote programming changes; Service of systems we did not install.\nSigns it is time: You find out a camera is down after an incident; Every repair starts with finding a vendor; No record of what was fixed and when; Firmware has never been updated.\nQ: Will you service equipment another company installed? A: Yes, after a takeover inspection where we test it and document what needs attention.\n\n# Building types (/buildings/)\n## Rental apartments (/buildings/multifamily/)\nFor: Owners, managing agents and supers of walk-ups, elevator buildings and new developments.\nTenants judge a building by the front door. We keep entry, cameras and fobs working, handle move-ins and move-outs, and give the super one number to call.\nTypical needs: Lobby entry that works with phones, not only buzzers; Cameras covering entrances, mailrooms and laundry; Fob changes when tenants move in and out; One vendor to call when something stops working.\nQ: Can you work around tenants? A: Yes. Common-area work is scheduled during the day with notices posted in advance. In-unit work is booked with each tenant.\nQ: Can the super manage fobs? A: Yes. The super gets an account limited to your building, and every change is logged.\n## Condos & co-ops (/buildings/condo/)\nFor: Boards, managing agents and resident managers.\nBoards want fewer complaints and clear reports. We plan upgrades around residents, document everything and send a summary the board can read in five minutes.\nTypical needs: Video entry and package access for residents; Reports the board can read in five minutes; A current certificate of insurance on file; Upgrades planned around residents, with notice.\nQ: Can you present to the board? A: Yes. We bring a short proposal with options and prices and answer questions at the meeting.\n## Private residences (/buildings/residence/)\nFor: Homeowners, townhouse and penthouse owners, architects and designers.\nA home that runs from one app and does not need a manual. We work with your architect from drawings and stay on call after you move in.\nTypical needs: A theater and whole-home audio run from one remote; Shades and lighting scenes by room and time of day; Whole-home Wi-Fi on a wired backbone; One app for door, cameras and climate.\nQ: When should we bring you in during a renovation? A: Before walls close: ideally at design development, so wiring, backing and conduit go in once.\n## Offices & commercial (/buildings/office/)\nFor: Office managers, facility teams, landlords and general contractors.\nMove-in day with working Wi-Fi, badges and conference rooms. We coordinate with your GC and IT, then handle moves, adds and changes.\nTypical needs: Cabling and Wi-Fi ready on move-in day; Badge access you can manage remotely; Conference rooms that connect on the first try; Moves, adds and changes without downtime.\nQ: Do you work with general contractors? A: Yes. We price from drawings, attend coordination meetings and close out with as-builts and test reports.\nQ: Can you work after hours? A: Yes. Cutovers and noisy work are scheduled at night or on weekends.\n## Retail & hospitality (/buildings/retail/)\nFor: Stores, restaurants, hotels and gyms.\nWatch the register from your phone, play the right music in each zone and keep guest Wi-Fi away from your payment network.\nTypical needs: Cameras on registers, stockrooms and doors; Background music and screens by zone; Guest Wi-Fi kept apart from the business network; Alarm you can arm and check from your phone.\nQ: Can you install before opening? A: Yes. Tell us the opening date and we schedule backwards from it.\nQ: Do you install in more than one location? A: Yes, with the same layout and one account for every store.\n## Schools & institutions (/buildings/institution/)\nFor: Schools and colleges, plus houses of worship, nonprofits and clinics.\nControlled entry, clear audio in every room and documentation ready for audits and grant reports.\nTypical needs: Controlled entry and visitor check-in; Public address and classroom AV; Network and Wi-Fi across the whole campus; Documentation for audits and grant reports.\nQ: Can you work during school breaks? A: Yes. Large installations are usually planned for summer and holidays.\nQ: Do you help with security grant paperwork? A: We provide equipment lists, quotes and documentation in the format the grant asks for.\n\n# Areas served (/areas/)\nBrooklyn: Our office is in Bensonhurst, so Brooklyn buildings get the shortest drive and the fastest dispatch. Neighborhoods include Bensonhurst, Bay Ridge, Dyker Heights, Borough Park, Midwood, Sheepshead Bay, Brighton Beach, Gravesend, Park Slope, Williamsburg, Downtown Brooklyn, Crown Heights.\nManhattan: Condos, co-ops, offices and townhouses from the Financial District to Washington Heights. Neighborhoods include Upper East Side, Upper West Side, Midtown, Chelsea, Tribeca, Financial District, Harlem, Washington Heights, Lower East Side, Murray Hill.\nQueens: Apartment buildings, retail strips and offices across Queens. Neighborhoods include Astoria, Long Island City, Forest Hills, Rego Park, Flushing, Jackson Heights, Bayside, Jamaica.\nStaten Island: Homes, stores and multifamily buildings across the island, a short drive over the Verrazzano. Neighborhoods include St. George, Tottenville, Great Kills, New Dorp, Todt Hill, Port Richmond.\nThe Bronx: Elevator buildings, schools and institutions across the Bronx. Neighborhoods include Riverdale, Fordham, Pelham Bay, Throgs Neck, Mott Haven, Parkchester.";
